---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../data/site';

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'Research with HailBrowser in your own logged-in tabs',
    description:
      'Ask a question, let HailBrowser open the sources in your browser, and get an answer-first report with numbered citations. Quick, Standard and Deep.',
    dateModified: '2026-10-30',
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'HailBrowser contributors' },
    mainEntityOfPage: `${SITE.url}/use-cases/web-research`,
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: `${SITE.url}/` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Use cases', item: `${SITE.url}/use-cases` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Web research', item: `${SITE.url}/use-cases/web-research` },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Web research from pages that need your login"
  description="HailBrowser research opens sources in background tabs of your own browser, so JavaScript-heavy and logged-in pages work, then writes an answer with numbered citations."
  type="article"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <div class="frame">
    <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/use-cases">Use cases</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span aria-current="page">Web research</span>
    </nav>

    <header class="page-hero">
      <div class="page-hero__meta">
        <span class="pill pill--ink">Use case 02</span>
        <span class="pill">Quick · Standard · Deep</span>
      </div>
      <h1>A report from the pages<br />a crawler cannot open.</h1>
      <p>
        Most research tools fetch a URL and hope. HailBrowser opens the source in a background tab of
        your browser, waits for it to render, and reads it with the session you already have.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="split split--wide-right">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>The prompt</h2>
        <CodeBlock
          code={`Research the current annual price of the three
password managers I have open, then tell me which
is cheapest per year and what each includes.
Cite every price.`}
          file="side panel · Research"
          lang="text"
          label="A question, a depth, a citation rule"
        />
        <p>
          Pick Quick for a first look, Standard for most decisions, Deep when the answer rests on
          several sources that disagree.
        </p>

        <h2>What happens</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Plan.</strong> Two to four search queries, written to cover the question from different angles.</li>
          <li><strong>Search.</strong> DuckDuckGo by default, with another engine tried if the first shows a CAPTCHA or nothing useful.</li>
          <li><strong>Read.</strong> The best results, different sites first, open in parallel background tabs of your browser. JavaScript, cookies and your logins all apply.</li>
          <li><strong>Fill gaps.</strong> Standard and Deep runs look for what is missing, or what rests on a single source, and search again.</li>
          <li><strong>Write.</strong> The report answers first, then gives detail. Every claim carries a numbered source, and citations to sources that do not exist are removed.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          Tabs are closed when it finishes. The report stays in the chat and in History, and can be
          copied, downloaded as Markdown, or repeated on a schedule.
        </p>

        <h2>What to check</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Open the numbers.</strong> Click a citation before you rely on it. The link should be the page the claim came from.</li>
          <li><strong>Look at the date.</strong> A price from last year is still a source; it is just not the current price.</li>
          <li><strong>Check the depth.</strong> A Quick run is a first look, not a decision document.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What can go wrong</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>The best sources are PDFs.</strong> Research cannot read PDFs yet. It will lean on HTML sources or return a thinner answer.</li>
          <li><strong>Every source is behind a login you do not have.</strong> It will report the gap instead of inventing an answer.</li>
          <li><strong>A site blocks automation.</strong> The run skips it and notes why; another source takes its place when one exists.</li>
          <li><strong>The question has no answer.</strong> It says so. That is the intended behaviour, not a failure.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <aside class="stack" style="align-content:start">
        <div class="callout">
          <p class="label">Depths</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Quick</strong> · 1 round · about 4 sources</li>
            <li><strong>Standard</strong> · 2 rounds · about 6</li>
            <li><strong>Deep</strong> · 3 rounds · about 10</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="callout">
          <p class="label">Tools in this guide</p>
          <ul class="tool-links">
            <li><a href="/docs/mcp#tool-tabs_create">tabs_create</a></li>
            <li><a href="/docs/mcp#tool-navigate">navigate</a></li>
            <li><a href="/docs/mcp#tool-read_page">read_page</a></li>
            <li><a href="/docs/mcp#tool-get_page_text">get_page_text</a></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="callout">
          <p class="label">Full mechanics</p>
          <p>
            The planning, reading and citation rules are in
            <a class="link" href="/docs/modes#research">the modes guide <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>

    <section class="section section--tight">
      <nav class="more-links" aria-label="Related">
        <a href="/use-cases/recurring-jobs">
          <span class="label">Next</span>
          <b>Recurring jobs</b>
          <em>The same report, every Monday morning.</em>
        </a>
        <a href="/docs/modes">
          <span class="label">Behaviour</span>
          <b>Modes</b>
          <em>Chat, Fast, Research and Tasks.</em>
        </a>
        <a href="/docs/limits">
          <span class="label">Honest</span>
          <b>Limits</b>
          <em>PDFs, paywalls and thin sources.</em>
        </a>
        <a href="/support">
          <span class="label">Help</span>
          <b>Support</b>
          <em>Report a source that failed.</em>
        </a>
      </nav>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
